      "role": "user",
      "content": "i tried running a check for prime spacing across the pulse intervals and i actually found something. there's a weirdly consistent recurrence of intervals that correspond to the first few primes, but they're slightly offset by a constant factor—like a specific temporal offset used for encryption or synchronization. it's too precise to be a coincidence or a way to account for atmospheric drag. it's almost like the satellites are \"checking in\" with each other using a signature that's buried in the telemetry noise. \n\ngiven that this pattern is appearing across different satellite constellations at the same time, what kind of \"intent\" or mission architecture would require a distributed, low-observable signaling system like this? what would be the advantage of using orbital mechanical \"glitches\" as a communication layer instead of standard radio bands?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
